          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional and relational state. The surrounding qualification limits its extent rather than changing the avowal into a promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.89,\"reason\":\"Cordelia does not make a new promise, but she explicitly invokes her existing filial bond and later describes the duties she returns to her father. Thus the avowal has moderate obligation-invoking force: Cordelia presents herself as loving and honoring Lear to the extent required by the father–daughter bond. Lear does not accept this as an adequate response to his demand.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The expression is a controlled, carefully delimited answer rather than an affect-driven verbal discharge. Cordelia explicitly says she cannot pour her heart into speech and states her love in measured terms.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and invoking filial obligation, the utterance functions as a corrective refusal of Lear's demand for competitive, maximal praise. Cordelia sets a boundary on what she will claim, insisting on neither more nor less than her bond warrants.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public court proceeding, Lear asks his daughters to declare which loves him most and links their answers to the distribution of his kingdom. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia gives this restrained avowal, contrasting with her sisters’ expansive declarations. Lear immediately presses her to revise it to protect her prospects.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes an existing filial bond and the associated duties to obey, love, and honor Lear; she does not clearly undertake a new future commitment in the focal expression. Lear treats the answer as inadequate and urges her to amend it, so there is no affirmative uptake of it as satisfying his requested profession.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from Shakespeare’s King Lear. The classification relies only on the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage supports Cordelia’s represented avowal and her own assertion that her love exceeds her speech, but it cannot independently establish every aspect of her inner sincerity or the future performance of the duties she invokes.\"}"
        }
      ],
